internal/clock/clock.go:111:24 replace 0 with -1 # equivalent: spendable := math.Max(0, R-M) at clock.go:111 becomes Max(-1, R-M), differing only when D := R-M < 0, where clean gives 0 and mutant gives -1. spendable is read only at clock.go:113 (feedforward = spendable/movesLeft + inc*share, and the bound scaling it) and at clock.go:117 as the argument of math.Max(spendable, SearchMinMoveTimeMs), the upper clamp of clamp(feedforward+corr, SearchMinMoveTimeMs, ...). Since both candidate values 0 and -1 are <= SearchMinMoveMs = config.SearchMinMoveTimeMs (10, and config's own tests internal/config/config_test.go:85,378 assert floor >= 1), the clamp bounds collapse to lo == hi == SearchMinMoveTimeMs for every input where the mutant differs, so clamp(anything, X, X) = X makes the returned budget identical; the pre-clamp sum may differ but is unobservable. The pid state is unaffected because err at clock.go:114 uses remainingMs and target only, never spendable, so all later Budget() calls match too, and Commit (clock.go:88-96) never reads law(). Verified empirically: a dense sweep (806 remaining values crossing the D=0 and D=1 boundaries in 0.5ms steps plus larger values, moves 0..45, 24 pid states covering primed/unprimed, prevErr in {-600,0,37,60880}, integ at both clamp edges and 0, all 3 time controls) compared clean vs mutated law outputs and post-step controller states: 0 diffs in ~5.4M pairs. Full ./internal/clock suite green with the mutant hand-applied. Re-keyed from clock.go:86:24 after the v0.21 Target export moved law() down 25 lines; the proof's substance is unchanged, the challenge audit re-verified it at the new position.
internal/clock/clock.go:111:24 replace 0 with 1 # equivalent: spendable := math.Max(0, R-M) at clock.go:111 becomes Max(1, R-M), differing only when D := R-M < 1, where clean gives 0 and mutant gives 1. Same clamp-collapse proof as the -1 variant: spendable is read only at clock.go:113 (feedforward/bound) and clock.go:117 (upper clamp math.Max(spendable, SearchMinMoveTimeMs)); both candidate values 0 and 1 are <= SearchMinMoveMs = 10 (config guarantees floor >= 1 via internal/config/config_test.go:85,378), so whenever the mutant differs, lo == hi == SearchMinMoveTimeMs and clamp collapses the possibly-different pre-clamp sum onto the same floor. pid state at clock.go:116 depends on err = remainingMs - target only, not spendable, so future budgets and commits are identical. Verified empirically by the same dense sweep (806 rem x 46 moves x 24 pid states x 3 tcs): 0 diffs in output or controller state for Max(1, D). Full ./internal/clock suite green with the mutant hand-applied. Re-keyed from clock.go:86:24 after the v0.21 Target export moved law() down 25 lines; the proof's substance is unchanged, the challenge audit re-verified it at the new position.
internal/clock/pid.go:39:14 replace 0 with -1 # equivalent: dead write. p.prevErr = 0 sits in reset() (pid.go:37-41) which also sets p.primed = false (pid.go:40). The only read of prevErr in the package is pid.go:30, guarded by `if p.primed`; every execution path from reset() to that read must pass through step(), whose first invocation writes p.prevErr = err (pid.go:32) before setting primed = true (pid.go:33), so the reset value can never be observed. No production caller exists (repo grep: the only call site is internal/clock/pid_test.go:97, whose post-reset assertions step first and so also cannot observe it), but even a future caller cannot: the write is structurally dead. Full ./internal/clock suite green with the mutant hand-applied.
